    "Apparent Power from Active and Reactive Power": "Apparent Power from Active and Reactive Power",
    "Apparent power": "Apparent power",
    "Applicable to sandy (cohesionless) soils; several variants of Dunham's formula exist for different grain shapes and gradations, so treat the result as an approximation. N should be the SPT blow count appropriate to the convention used locally.": "Applicable to sandy (cohesionless) soils; several variants of Dunham's formula exist for different grain shapes and gradations, so treat the result as an approximation. N should be the SPT blow count appropriate to the convention used locally.",
    "Applies the ReLU (rectified linear unit) activation, max(0, x), passing positive pre-activations through unchanged while zeroing out negative ones. It is the most widely used activation function in deep neural networks for its simplicity and effectiveness.": "Applies the ReLU (rectified linear unit) activation, max(0, x), passing positive pre-activations through unchanged while zeroing out negative ones. It is the most widely used activation function in deep neural networks for its simplicity and effectiveness.",
    "Assumes deep water (depth much greater than wavelength) and a narrow-banded sea spectrum. Power grows with the square of wave height, so larger swells dominate the available resource.": "Assumes deep water (depth much greater than wavelength) and a narrow-banded sea spectrum. Power grows with the square of wave height, so larger swells dominate the available resource.",
    "Assumes every cell divides synchronously with no cell death. n is typically a non-negative integer; n=0 gives the single starting cell.": "Assumes every cell divides synchronously with no cell death. n is typically a non-negative integer; n=0 gives the single starting cell.",
    "Assumes free fall from rest with no air resistance. h and g must use consistent units (e.g., meters and m/s²) for t to come out in seconds.": "Assumes free fall from rest with no air resistance. h and g must use consistent units (e.g., meters and m/s²) for t to come out in seconds.",
