    "Effective Half-Life": "Effective Half-Life",
    "Effective depth": "Effective depth",
    "Efficiency is always below 100% for real machines because of friction and other losses. Pout and Pin must be measured in the same power unit.": "Efficiency is always below 100% for real machines because of friction and other losses. Pout and Pin must be measured in the same power unit.",
    "Electric Field of a Point Charge": "Electric Field of a Point Charge",
    "Enter the appropriate coefficient for the conversion needed: an L-value greater than 1 for loose/hauled volume, or a C-value less than 1 for compacted/finished volume. Coefficients are soil-specific and should come from geotechnical data or local earthwork standards.": "Enter the appropriate coefficient for the conversion needed: an L-value greater than 1 for loose/hauled volume, or a C-value less than 1 for compacted/finished volume. Coefficients are soil-specific and should come from geotechnical data or local earthwork standards.",
    "Enter variation and deviation as signed values (east positive, west negative, by convention). If the true heading falls outside 0-360, add or subtract 360 as needed.": "Enter variation and deviation as signed values (east positive, west negative, by convention). If the true heading falls outside 0-360, add or subtract 360 as needed.",
    "Epicenter Distance from S-P Interval (Omori's Formula)": "Epicenter Distance from S-P Interval (Omori's Formula)",
    "Equivalent to computing (a+b)^2 directly; this form shows the expanded terms explicitly, as taught in junior-high algebra.": "Equivalent to computing (a+b)^2 directly; this form shows the expanded terms explicitly, as taught in junior-high algebra.",
    "Estimates a laser diode's optical output power above lasing threshold, P_out = eta_slope*(I - I_threshold), using the slope efficiency and drive current above the threshold current. Below threshold the laser emits negligible coherent light.": "Estimates a laser diode's optical output power above lasing threshold, P_out = eta_slope*(I - I_threshold), using the slope efficiency and drive current above the threshold current. Below threshold the laser emits negligible coherent light.",
    "Estimates a single-axis tilt angle from an accelerometer reading, theta = asin(ax/g), the angle between that sensor axis and horizontal, assuming the sensor measures only the static gravity component along that axis.": "Estimates a single-axis tilt angle from an accelerometer reading, theta = asin(ax/g), the angle between that sensor axis and horizontal, assuming the sensor measures only the static gravity component along that axis.",
    "Estimates electrical power output from a geothermal plant, P = m*cp*(Tin-Tout)*eta, from the thermal energy extracted from the geothermal fluid (mass flow, specific heat, and temperature drop) scaled by the plant's conversion efficiency.": "Estimates electrical power output from a geothermal plant, P = m*cp*(Tin-Tout)*eta, from the thermal energy extracted from the geothermal fluid (mass flow, specific heat, and temperature drop) scaled by the plant's conversion efficiency.",
    "Estimates the allowable bearing capacity of sandy soil from its Standard Penetration Test blow count N, using the simplified empirical relation qa≈30+0.6·N. It gives a quick preliminary check before a full geotechnical analysis.": "Estimates the allowable bearing capacity of sandy soil from its Standard Penetration Test blow count N, using the simplified empirical relation qa≈30+0.6·N. It gives a quick preliminary check before a full geotechnical analysis.",
    "Estimates the distance from a seismograph station to an earthquake epicenter using Omori's formula, k*(ts-tp), from the S-P arrival time interval. The larger the delay between P- and S-wave arrivals, the farther the epicenter.": "Estimates the distance from a seismograph station to an earthquake epicenter using Omori's formula, k*(ts-tp), from the S-P arrival time interval. The larger the delay between P- and S-wave arrivals, the farther the epicenter.",
    "Estimates the hourly production rate of earthmoving equipment such as excavators or loaders from the load capacity per cycle q, a soil/material conversion factor f, a work efficiency factor Ew, and the cycle time Cm in minutes.": "Estimates the hourly production rate of earthmoving equipment such as excavators or loaders from the load capacity per cycle q, a soil/material conversion factor f, a work efficiency factor Ew, and the cycle time Cm in minutes.",
    "Estimates the internal friction angle φ of sandy soil from its Standard Penetration Test blow count N using Dunham's formula, φ=sqrt(15·N)+15. It provides a quick empirical friction angle for use in bearing capacity or earth pressure calculations.": "Estimates the internal friction angle φ of sandy soil from its Standard Penetration Test blow count N using Dunham's formula, φ=sqrt(15·N)+15. It provides a quick empirical friction angle for use in bearing capacity or earth pressure calculations.",
    "Estimates the lateral load transferred between the inside and outside wheels during cornering, deltaW = (m*ay*h)/t, from vehicle mass, lateral acceleration, centre-of-gravity height, and track width.": "Estimates the lateral load transferred between the inside and outside wheels during cornering, deltaW = (m*ay*h)/t, from vehicle mass, lateral acceleration, centre-of-gravity height, and track width.",
    "Estimates the minimum length of a spiral transition curve using Shortt's formula, from the design speed V, curve radius R, and an allowable rate of change of radial (centripetal) acceleration C.": "Estimates the minimum length of a spiral transition curve using Shortt's formula, from the design speed V, curve radius R, and an allowable rate of change of radial (centripetal) acceleration C.",
    "Estimates the number of histogram bins to use for a dataset of size n with Sturges' rule, k = 1 + log2(n). It gives a simple, commonly used starting point for choosing bin counts.": "Estimates the number of histogram bins to use for a dataset of size n with Sturges' rule, k = 1 + log2(n). It gives a simple, commonly used starting point for choosing bin counts.",
    "Estimates the return period of a flood event using the Weibull plotting-position formula, T = (n+1)/m, from the length of the flow record n and the rank m of the event (1 for the largest flood on record).": "Estimates the return period of a flood event using the Weibull plotting-position formula, T = (n+1)/m, from the length of the flow record n and the rank m of the event (1 for the largest flood on record).",
    "Estimates the steady-state rate of descent during a power-off glide from the true airspeed TAS and the lift-to-drag ratio LD, using the standard aviation approximation ROD = TAS × 101.3 / (L/D).": "Estimates the steady-state rate of descent during a power-off glide from the true airspeed TAS and the lift-to-drag ratio LD, using the standard aviation approximation ROD = TAS × 101.3 / (L/D).",
    "Estimates the voltage drop along a single-phase two-wire feeder or cable run from the load current I, the cable's resistance R and reactance X per conductor, and the power-factor angle θ, using the simplified approximate formula e = 2I(Rcosθ + Xsinθ).": "Estimates the voltage drop along a single-phase two-wire feeder or cable run from the load current I, the cable's resistance R and reactance X per conductor, and the power-factor angle θ, using the simplified approximate formula e = 2I(Rcosθ + Xsinθ).",
    "Ew accounts for operator skill, job-site conditions, and management delays (typically 0.5-0.9); f converts between bank, loose, and compacted volume states. This is the standard production-rate formula used in earthmoving equipment estimating.": "Ew accounts for operator skill, job-site conditions, and management delays (typically 0.5-0.9); f converts between bank, loose, and compacted volume states. This is the standard production-rate formula used in earthmoving equipment estimating.",
