    "Converts a solution's mass percent concentration into molarity (mol/L) using the solution density and the solute's molar mass. This is the standard relationship connecting weight-based and volume-based concentration units in analytical and physical chemistry.": "Converts a solution's mass percent concentration into molarity (mol/L) using the solution density and the solute's molar mass. This is the standard relationship connecting weight-based and volume-based concentration units in analytical and physical chemistry.",
    "Cycloid Arc Length (One Arch)": "Cycloid Arc Length (One Arch)",
    "Deflection of a Charged Particle in a Uniform Electric Field (Parallel Plates)": "Deflection of a Charged Particle in a Uniform Electric Field (Parallel Plates)",
    "Density d must be in g/mL and mass percent w in %; the factor 10 converts these units into consistent mol/L output. Density and mass percent should be measured at the same temperature.": "Density d must be in g/mL and mass percent w in %; the factor 10 converts these units into consistent mol/L output. Density and mass percent should be measured at the same temperature.",
    "Derives the frequency of the recessive O allele (i) from the observed frequency of the O blood type phenotype, under Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. Since the O phenotype arises only from the homozygous ii genotype (frequency r²), the allele frequency r is obtained by taking the square root of the phenotype frequency.": "Derives the frequency of the recessive O allele (i) from the observed frequency of the O blood type phenotype, under Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. Since the O phenotype arises only from the homozygous ii genotype (frequency r²), the allele frequency r is obtained by taking the square root of the phenotype frequency.",
    "Determines the mass percent of carbon in an organic sample from the mass of carbon dioxide produced by complete combustion, based on the fact that all carbon in the sample is converted to CO2.": "Determines the mass percent of carbon in an organic sample from the mass of carbon dioxide produced by complete combustion, based on the fact that all carbon in the sample is converted to CO2.",
    "Determines the mass percent of hydrogen in an organic sample from the mass of water produced by complete combustion, based on the fact that all hydrogen in the sample is converted to H2O.": "Determines the mass percent of hydrogen in an organic sample from the mass of water produced by complete combustion, based on the fact that all hydrogen in the sample is converted to H2O.",
    "Determines the molar mass of a nonvolatile, non-dissociating solute from the observed boiling point elevation of a solution, using the ebullioscopic (boiling point elevation) constant of the solvent. This colligative-property method is analogous to the freezing point depression method.": "Determines the molar mass of a nonvolatile, non-dissociating solute from the observed boiling point elevation of a solution, using the ebullioscopic (boiling point elevation) constant of the solvent. This colligative-property method is analogous to the freezing point depression method.",
    "Determines the molar mass of a nonvolatile, non-dissociating solute from the observed freezing point depression of a solution, using the cryoscopic (freezing point depression) constant of the solvent. This is a classic colligative-property method for molecular weight determination.": "Determines the molar mass of a nonvolatile, non-dissociating solute from the observed freezing point depression of a solution, using the cryoscopic (freezing point depression) constant of the solvent. This is a classic colligative-property method for molecular weight determination.",
    "Determines the velocity of one fragment produced when a single body at a known velocity splits into two pieces (e.g. an explosion or decay), given the mass and resulting velocity of the other fragment. It is derived from conservation of momentum for the system before and after separation.": "Determines the velocity of one fragment produced when a single body at a known velocity splits into two pieces (e.g. an explosion or decay), given the mass and resulting velocity of the other fragment. It is derived from conservation of momentum for the system before and after separation.",
