    "Urine concentration of substance X": "Urine concentration of substance X",
    "Use consistent units: c in mol/L, M in g/mol, d in g/mL. The density value must correspond to the same solution whose concentration is being converted.": "Use consistent units: c in mol/L, M in g/mol, d in g/mL. The density value must correspond to the same solution whose concentration is being converted.",
    "Uses average bond energies and is therefore an approximation, less accurate than using heats of formation; strictly applies only to reactions where all species are in the gas phase. Coefficients (n1-n4) represent moles of each bond type broken or formed per mole of reaction, not molecule counts.": "Uses average bond energies and is therefore an approximation, less accurate than using heats of formation; strictly applies only to reactions where all species are in the gas phase. Coefficients (n1-n4) represent moles of each bond type broken or formed per mole of reaction, not molecule counts.",
    "Uses the sign convention where heat added to the system (Qh) is positive and work done by the system (W) is positive; applies to a closed system with no change in the amount of substance.": "Uses the sign convention where heat added to the system (Qh) is positive and work done by the system (W) is positive; applies to a closed system with no change in the amount of substance.",
    "Valid for a simple salt of a weak monoacidic base and a strong acid, in the concentration range where the approximation [H+] ≈ sqrt(Kw·C/Kb) holds (C should not be too dilute, roughly C >= 10^-3 mol/L). pKw = 14 corresponds to 25°C.": "Valid for a simple salt of a weak monoacidic base and a strong acid, in the concentration range where the approximation [H+] ≈ sqrt(Kw·C/Kb) holds (C should not be too dilute, roughly C >= 10^-3 mol/L). pKw = 14 corresponds to 25°C.",
    "Valid for a simple salt of a weak monoprotic acid and a strong base, in the concentration range where the approximation [OH-] ≈ sqrt(Kw·C/Ka) holds (C should not be too dilute, roughly C >= 10^-3 mol/L). pKw = 14 corresponds to 25°C.": "Valid for a simple salt of a weak monoprotic acid and a strong base, in the concentration range where the approximation [OH-] ≈ sqrt(Kw·C/Ka) holds (C should not be too dilute, roughly C >= 10^-3 mol/L). pKw = 14 corresponds to 25°C.",
    "Valid for any random variable X with finite mean mu = E(X); a and b are constants and mu must already be computed or known beforehand.": "Valid for any random variable X with finite mean mu = E(X); a and b are constants and mu must already be computed or known beforehand.",
    "Valid for any random variable X with finite variance; sigma must be entered as the standard deviation of X, not the variance itself.": "Valid for any random variable X with finite variance; sigma must be entered as the standard deviation of X, not the variance itself.",
    "Valid for n ≥ 1; the formula correctly reduces to a1 when n = 1. Here b1 is the first term of the difference sequence (b1 = a2 − a1).": "Valid for n ≥ 1; the formula correctly reduces to a1 when n = 1. Here b1 is the first term of the difference sequence (b1 = a2 − a1).",
    "Valid for one-dimensional perfectly inelastic collisions only; the result is always non-negative since kinetic energy can only decrease (or stay the same) in such a collision.": "Valid for one-dimensional perfectly inelastic collisions only; the result is always non-negative since kinetic energy can only decrease (or stay the same) in such a collision.",
    "Valid only for a quasi-static (reversible) process at strictly constant pressure; for processes where pressure varies, this must be replaced by the integral of P dV.": "Valid only for a quasi-static (reversible) process at strictly constant pressure; for processes where pressure varies, this must be replaced by the integral of P dV.",
    "Valid only for reactions that are truly second order overall (second order in a single reactant, or first order in each of two reactants with equal initial concentrations). The time unit of the result matches whatever time unit is implicit in the rate constant k.": "Valid only for reactions that are truly second order overall (second order in a single reactant, or first order in each of two reactants with equal initial concentrations). The time unit of the result matches whatever time unit is implicit in the rate constant k.",
    "Valid only for small oscillation angles (typically under about 15°), where sin(θ) ≈ θ; treats the pendulum as a point mass on a massless, inextensible string.": "Valid only for small oscillation angles (typically under about 15°), where sin(θ) ≈ θ; treats the pendulum as a point mass on a massless, inextensible string.",
    "Valid only when the compound contains no elements other than C, H, and O; any nitrogen, sulfur, halogens, etc. present would be incorrectly counted as oxygen. Experimental error in the C and H determinations propagates directly into this value.": "Valid only when the compound contains no elements other than C, H, and O; any nitrogen, sulfur, halogens, etc. present would be incorrectly counted as oxygen. Experimental error in the C and H determinations propagates directly into this value.",
    "Valid only when the two genes are linked on the same chromosome (a recombination frequency of 50% indicates independent assortment or genes far apart); assumes offspring counts are large enough to be statistically reliable.": "Valid only when the two genes are linked on the same chromosome (a recombination frequency of 50% indicates independent assortment or genes far apart); assumes offspring counts are large enough to be statistically reliable.",
